We then have the
isomorphism
\begin{align*}
  \mathrm{Pic}(G)&\simeq \mathrm{cok}(L)=\mathbb{Z}^n/\mathrm{im}_{\mathbb{Z}}(L)\\
  \textstyle [\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_iv_i]&\mapsto(a_1,\dots,a_n)+\mathrm{im}_{\mathbb{Z}}(L).
\end{align*}

The divisor~$D$ is~{\em $q$-reduced} if
\begin{enumerate}
  \item[(i)] $D(v)\geq 0$ for all~$v\in V\setminus\left\{ q \right\}$, and 
  \item[(ii)]  $D$ has no legal set firing by a nonempty set $W\subseteq V\setminus\left\{ q \right\}$.
\end{enumerate}

Let~$C_n$ be the cycle graph on~$n$ vertices, with
vertices~$v_1,\dots,v_n$ around the cycle.  Take~$q=v_n$. It is well-known
that~$\mathrm{Jac}(C_n)\simeq\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z}$, with generator~$D_1:=[v_1-q]$ and such
that $D_j:=[v_{j}-q]=j[v_1-q]$ for all~$j$, where the indices are determined
modulo~$n$.  Therefore,~$\mathrm{ord}_{q}(v_i)=n/\gcd(i,n)$ for all~$i$.  For
convenience, take~$\ell_{v_i}=n$ for~$i=1,\dots,n-1$ and~$\ell_q=1$.  The reduced
Laplacian~$\tilde{L}$ is the~$(n-1)\times(n-1)$ tridiagonal matrix with~$2$s on the
diagonal and~$-1$s on the super and subdiagonals.  It is straightforward to
reduce~$\tilde{L}$ to its Hermite form, which is~$I_{n-1}+B_{n-1}$ where~$I_{n-1}$ is the
identity matrix and~$B_{n-1}$ is a matrix whose rows are all~$0$-vectors except for
the last row, which is the vector~$(1,2,\dots,n-1)$.  See Figure~\ref{fig: ps-cycle} for an example.
