Let~$K_n$ be the
complete graph with vertex set~$V=\{v_1,\dots,v_n\}$, and let~$q:=v_n$. Its reduced
Laplacian~$\tilde{L}$ is the matrix~$nI_{n-1}-J_{n-1}$ where~$J_{n-1}$ is
the~$(n-1)\times(n-1)$ matrix whose entries are all~$1$. The inverse
is~$\tilde{L}^{-1}=\frac{1}{n}(I_{n-1}+J_{n-1})$, and therefore,~$\mathrm{ord}_{q}(v)=n$ for all
vertices~$v\neq q$. To reduce~$\tilde{L}$ to Hermite normal form, add
columns~$2$ through~$n-1$ to the first column of~$\tilde{L}$,
then add the first column to each of the others.  The result is the matrix
formed by replacing the first column of~$nI_{n-1}$ by a column of all~$1$s. So
the image of~$\tilde{L}$ in~$(\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z})^{n-1}$ is spanned by the vector of all~$1$s.
See Figure~\ref{fig: ps-complete} for an example.  
